diff --git a/build/vulkan-ios/README.md b/build/vulkan-ios/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..54694ff762f6fef2e8ce14d51d2c2dcba47ccd41
--- /dev/null
+++ b/build/vulkan-ios/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,350 @@
+# Experimental iOS Vulkan milestone backend
+
+This local integration targets Windows x64, Vulkan 1.1 instance creation,
+real physical-device enumeration, and an offscreen image-clear/readback test.
+Earlier local builds passed M1, M2 and two visible RGB presentation rounds on a real Apple M5 iPad. The current source-built MoltenVK cache-isolation change requires new real-device verification.
+It is not a claim of game compatibility or complete Vulkan support.
+
+## Build after the normal documented prerequisites
+
+```sh
+bash build/vulkan-ios/fetch.sh
+bash build/vulkan-ios/build-moltenvk-source.sh
+bash build/vulkan-ios/build.sh
+JOBS=2 bash build/wine-pe/build-modules.sh vulkan-1 winevulkan
+bash build/win32u-unix/build.sh
+bash build/ntdll-unix/build.sh
+xcodebuild -project app/Madeira.xcodeproj -scheme Madeira -configuration Debug \
+  -destination 'generic/platform=iOS' -derivedDataPath ../DerivedData -jobs 2 \
+  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY= DEVELOPMENT_TEAM= build
+```
+
+Run the Vulkan PE build AFTER tools/build-stub-dlls.py, which would overwrite
+the real vulkan-1.dll. The added archive is required by this local Xcode project.
+The Vulkan headers are pinned to the official v1.4.2 iOS archive, verified by SHA-256.
+The runtime is source-built from MoltenVK commit
+`db66022459ffb663aa2b50f6b018bc2e124f5edf` plus the checked-in vertex-layout
+cache patch. `build.sh` requires and checks the resulting archive and patch hashes.
+The original prebuilt archive is retained separately. The source build follows
+upstream fetchDependencies and Xcode packaging; it needs Xcode and Git.
+For an existing pinned source checkout, set `MADEIRA_MOLTENVK_SOURCE` to its path.
+Its license is copied to the app's legal directory. A full clean-machine build
+has not been verified.
+
+## Paths
+
+Windows test -> Wine vulkan-1 export forward -> WineVulkan ARM64EC loader /
+generated thunk -> ntdll's named WineVulkan Unix table -> native WineVulkan ->
+win32u Vulkan driver -> static MoltenVK symbols -> Metal driver / GPU on iPad.
+The Windows DLL retains Wine's AMD64 PE header and ARM64EC CHPE metadata;
+llvm-readobj recognizes it as COFF-ARM64EC.
+
+Wine's win32u creates and caches wrappers for host physical devices during
+instance creation. Guest vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices returns that host-derived
+cached list; it does not issue another native enumeration on each call.
+
+Only binding and native symbol resolution are adapted for iOS. Native results,
+feature bits, device identities and counts are not synthesized. A real driver's
+mandatory VK_KHR_portability_subset is enabled on the native device when
+reported, without adding optional feature bits. This follows VkDeviceCreateInfo
+VUID-pProperties-04451; the Windows-facing extension filter remains Wine's.
+
+## Tests and limits
+
+`python3 tests/host/check-vulkan-static.py` compiles backend.c against a fixture
+and checks failure propagation, missing entry points, and portability handling.
+It is NOT GPU evidence. `tests/x64/vulkan-smoke.c` builds two Windows x64 programs.
+The second records a 16x16 RGBA8 magenta clear, copies into a mapped buffer,
+waits for a fence, invalidates memory and compares every one of 1024 bytes.
+
+Window presentation now selects the local iOS Metal surface driver in
+`build/win32u-unix/vulkan_surface_ios.c` instead of Wine's null/headless driver.
+It maps the real native VK_EXT_metal_surface to the Windows WSI extension,
+retains the existing HWND layer via IOSDisplayShim, and propagates native
+vkCreateMetalSurfaceEXT results. Wine retains swapchain and window lifecycle
+management; Winios/MetalHostView retain their existing layer geometry updates.
+A presentation-support query uses an actual temporary Metal surface plus the
+native vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceSupportKHR, never a constant VK_TRUE.
+
+`python3 tests/host/check-vulkan-surface.py` exercises the real C adapter and
+actual driver entry against external API fixtures. It tests extension mapping,
+failed allocation/missing layer/native errors, balanced references, and native
+presentation support. This cannot prove UIKit behavior or visible output.
+
+`tests/x64/vulkan-present.c` is an ordinary Windows x64 Vulkan WSI program:
+it creates an HWND, requests Win32 surface/swapchain, verifies transfer support,
+clears acquired images RED/GREEN/BLUE, and submits/presents/waits. It repeats
+with a second HWND of a different size and destroys each surface/swapchain.
+An API PASS log still requires visible output confirmation on the iPad.
+Build with the same pinned x64 clang and MoltenVK headers as the smoke tests,
+plus `-luser32`.
+
+The UI FPS counter currently measures the existing winemetal path; it is not
+proof of Vulkan presentation or lack thereof. No fake counter increments are
+added here. Native vkQueuePresentKHR status is logged (first 8, every 120,
+and all nonzero results), preserving both overall and per-swapchain results.
+WoW64 Vulkan is not registered. Extended pNext feature support, shaders,
+rendering pipelines, swapchains and game compatibility remain unverified.
+The test prints the actual Windows-visible instance/device extensions and all
+55 Vulkan 1.0 core feature booleans. Device-specific unsupported capabilities
+must be assessed from the real-iPad log, not inferred from the compiled library.
+
+
+## Optional failure-only graphics-pipeline diagnostics
+
+`env.MADEIRA_VK_PIPELINE_DIAGNOSTICS = 1` enables the observer in
+`pipeline_diagnostics.h`. It wraps the native shader/module lifetime and graphics
+pipeline calls, preserving native requests, allocator callbacks and results.
+Before native pipeline creation it captures bounded vertex/stage/specialization
+state and retains exact shader bytes. Only negative native call results export
+up to eight member snapshots with call/thread IDs, plus SHA-256-named shader
+files under `MADEIRA_DOCS_DIR/vulkan-diagnostics/run-*`.
+
+The budget covers retained shader/module records and snapshots (64 MiB).
+Success and positive compile-required results produce no failure files. Module
+metadata is retired before the native handle can be recycled; retained snapshots
+keep referenced bytes alive. No registry or I/O mutex is held across native
+Vulkan calls. The iOS public vm_read_overwrite reads observer data without
+faulting on unavailable/ignored pointers. Read errors, bounded fields and unknown
+pNext payloads are explicitly incomplete observations, never invented state.
+
+Disable `MVK_CONFIG_SHADER_DUMP_DIR` when using this observer to avoid repeating
+the large full-shader dump. This is opt-in diagnostic instrumentation, not a fix
+for pipeline compilation or a claim of another real-device pass. Host contract
+checks: `python3 tests/host/check-vulkan-pipeline-diagnostics.py`.
+
+## Vertex-layout cache isolation
+
+`patches/0001-isolate-vertex-input-cache.patch` isolates incomplete vertex-stage
+conversion inputs. A configuration without a record for an input consumed by
+the actual shader could otherwise match another layout; `alignWith()` then
+cleared the new layout's usage flags and omitted its Metal attribute descriptor.
+
+The original full-layout isolation also unnecessarily split valid shader variants
+when layouts differed only in unused attributes. The revised patch derives a
+`vertexInputsFullyCovered` hint after real SPIRV-Cross conversion. Simple active
+scalar/vector inputs must all have mappings before used-input matching is allowed.
+Missing, complex, component-qualified, and imported/unknown configurations retain
+conservative full-layout matching. The hint is deliberately not serialized:
+loaded cache records default to conservative matching, preserving the existing
+cache format and preventing old incomplete records from gaining trusted status.
+
+This does not add attributes, change shaders, enable features, replace results,
+or suppress errors. Non-vertex matching is unchanged. The native regression
+`tests/vulkan/vertex-cache-reuse-probe.c` checks that adding an unused location
+reuses the MoltenVK shader-library cache (771 -> 771 bytes, previously 771 ->
+1535), while invalid primers still cannot poison later valid pipelines.
+`vertex-converter-probe.cpp` exercises the real converter and matching methods.
+This is an implementation-specific cache regression, not a Vulkan requirement
+that every driver's exported cache size stay constant. No Detroit success or
+iPad speedup follows from these native Mac controls alone.
+
+`tests/x64/vulkan-vertex-cache.c` is an independent Vulkan regression program.
+It intentionally primes a cache using layouts missing the shader's location 3,
+then compares correctly supplied location-3 pipelines with a fresh-cache control.
+The primer calls violate VUID-VkGraphicsPipelineCreateInfo-Input-07904 unless
+optional maintenance9/vertexAttributeRobustness is enabled; the test makes no
+claim that those calls must succeed. This is a defensive compatibility change
+for failure isolation, not a claim that the primer is valid Vulkan.
+
+The test runs natively on macOS and as a Windows x64 PE that loads vulkan-1.dll
+through LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress. All tested pipeline creation uses Vulkan.
+The final `[vk-vertex-cache-test] PASS` requires real VK_SUCCESS from each
+subsequent complete layout and the control. Device verification is still needed
+after the native Mac regression; a Mac result is not an iPad result.
+
+## Additional verified diagnostic and session fixes
+
+`0002-warn-only-if-integer-blending-enabled.patch` corrects a MoltenVK warning
+that claimed blending was enabled for unsupported integer attachments even
+when blendEnable was false. It changes only the warning condition; native
+pipeline/rendering behavior and actual unsupported-blending warnings remain.
+`tests/vulkan/check-integer-blend-native.py INCLUDE_DIR LIBMOLTENVK.dylib`
+compiles and runs a real R32_UINT pipeline with blending disabled/enabled.
+
+The library now latches completion of the launched initial process, including
+normal exits, independently of the existing crash status. This avoids treating
+a program that exits between 0.5-second polls as a failed launch. It does not
+turn a crash into success: finish() still reads the original NTSTATUS report.
+The latch resets at session start; the initial-process exit hook continues to
+exclude children/helpers. `check-fast-session-exit.py` exercises the actual C
+latch and actual Swift launchFailed/poll branches; this is not UIKit/iPad proof.
+
+
+## Failure-boundary / direct-cache-export follow-up
+
+The local source build now verifies a third patch, `0003-size-cache-before-direct-export.patch`:
+cache serialization calculates the required size before either size queries or direct data reads.
+Native macOS empty/populated-cache controls fail before and pass after the patch; the iOS result
+is still pending a real-device run. This does not establish that it fixes Detroit's black screen.
+
+`MADEIRA_VK_PIPELINE_DIAGNOSTICS=1` also enables bounded PE-entry and Unix-entry fingerprints
+of pipeline flags and static vertex-input bindings/attributes. PE output needs `trace+vulkan`.
+Only negative native results are logged (first32); unknown extension chains/failed safe reads
+are incomplete and cannot be compared as valid fingerprints. No arguments, outputs, errors or
+GPU capabilities are rewritten. See `tests/host/check-vulkan-{vertex-stamp,pe-boundary}.py`.
+The x64 combined regression additionally runs `vulkan-cache-export.c` with valid synthetic shaders.
+
+## Bounded optional pipeline-cache residency (iOS memory follow-up)
+
+`0004-bound-pipeline-shader-cache.patch` limits each iOS `VkPipelineCache`
+to 256 resident shader-library units by default. A base library and each
+macro-specialized variant each consume one unit; empty/failed shader groups
+consume one unit. Least-recently-used complete shader-module groups are evicted
+until within the limit. This is a count limit, not a byte limit or a bound on
+all application/Metal memory. Multiple VkPipelineCache objects have separate
+budgets. Direct MSL libraries and shader-module-local caches are unchanged.
+
+`env.MADEIRA_VK_SHADER_CACHE_MAX_LIBRARIES = 0` restores unlimited caching;
+a positive unsigned 32-bit value overrides the limit for the next process.
+Invalid input uses the platform default. Non-iOS platforms retain unlimited
+caching unless explicitly overridden. The policy does not change shader bytes,
+features, capabilities, Vulkan results, or live Vulkan pipelines. Eviction may
+cause additional compilation on later misses and may reduce exported cache data.
+
+The cache now returns an independently retained `MVKMTLFunction` under the same
+cache lock used for lookup. It evicts only after that function is retained;
+borrowed shader-library pointers cannot escape the lock and race eviction.
+Import and merge also enforce the limit. The cache format/UUID is unchanged.
+`0005-account-for-nonserialized-cache-hint.patch` corrects the debug Cereal
+size validator for the earlier intentionally nonserialized coverage hint and
+its padding; it does not add that hint to the serialized format.
+
+Each cache logs its actual library limit and effective source-compression enum;
+eviction summaries are limited to the first four and every 512th group.
+Upstream `MVK_CONFIG_SHADER_COMPRESSION_ALGORITHM` remains independently
+configurable, with its original default. No compression benefit on the iPad
+is assumed.
+
+`tests/x64/vulkan-cache-budget.c` creates/destroys 3x the selected number of
+unique compute pipelines and checks exported-cache size plateaus. It keeps
+an early pipeline alive across eviction, dispatches it and reads back the GPU's
+actual written word, then checks recreation, four concurrent cache users,
+export/import and merge. It is a native Mac and Windows x64 test. The optional
+first argument is the expected library limit (default256). It requires a real
+Vulkan driver; passing on Mac does not prove the Windows/iPad path.
+
+Real iPad JetsamEvent for PID4306 at 2026-10-18 04:45:38 confirmed
+`per-process-limit` with a peak of 12288.08 MiB. This policy addresses one
+reproduced source of retained compilation memory. It does not establish that
+all of the game's memory growth or black screen is fixed. Existing unsupported
+Vulkan features remain unsupported. The new IPA still needs device verification.
+
+### Merged macro-specialization lifetime
+
+`0006-own-merged-specialization-libraries.patch` fixes shallow copying of
+`MVKShaderLibrary::_specializationVariants`. Raw pointers in both caches
+previously referred to the same owned variant objects, so destroying the source
+cache invalidated variants in the destination. Each copied library now owns
+independent variant wrappers through unique_ptr and recursively rebinds their
+owner to the destination cache; immutable Metal libraries retain their normal
+Objective-C ownership. Copy assignment preserves the same value semantics.
+
+`tests/x64/vulkan-cache-specialization.c` uses a valid storage-buffer compute
+shader with a specialization-sized workgroup array (macro specialization).
+It verifies a non-default value 6 on the GPU before merge, after merge, and
+after destroying the source cache. The old library passes before source
+destruction but fails pipeline creation afterwards; the fix preserves actual
+readback. The supplied run4306 log contains no vkMergePipelineCaches call, so
+this independently reproduced bug is not established as the cause of that run.
+
+`tests/x64/vulkan-memory-regression.c` combines both new tests into one Windows
+program. It performs GPU compute/readback without a visible color screen.
+`tests/vulkan/check-cache-memory-native.py HEADERS LIBMOLTENVK.dylib` runs the
+native equivalent at limits1/8/256. iPad results for the new tests remain pending.
+
+## Optional bounded Metal compiler-input observer
+
+`patches/0007-observe-metal-library-compile-inputs.patch` adds a default-off
+observer at the real `newLibraryWithSource:options:completionHandler:` call.
+`env.MADEIRA_VK_SHADER_COMPILE_DIAGNOSTICS = 1` enables it for the next process.
+It fingerprints the actual MSL source, Metal device identity, language/math/
+invariance/optimization options set by the pinned implementation, and sorted
+actual preprocessor macro names, numeric types and values. It does not export
+source text, retain Metal objects, alter arguments/results, or schedule work.
+
+A fixed 32768-entry ledger occupies about 1 MiB. Each lookup holds its own
+mutex for at most 64 probes; no observer mutex is held across Metal compilation.
+Unknown inputs after collision/saturation are explicitly `untracked`, not
+counted as unique. SHA-256 fingerprints are observations, not a formal proof
+of source equivalence. Device pointers are stable for these runs; pointer
+reuse after device destruction is a limitation of cross-lifetime statistics.
+
+`[vk-compiler]` reports the first completion and every 256 completions, with
+unique/repeated/untracked counts, failed library completions and summed elapsed
+milliseconds by category. Durations run from just before the actual Metal call
+to its callback, including synchronous work and asynchronous wait. They exclude
+earlier dispatch/synchronized-lock wait and are not CPU time or process wall
+time; concurrent totals overlap. The observer records actual callbacks, so a
+Vulkan-side timeout followed by a late callback is not an API-success counter.
+Macro-specialized recompiles are observed too, using their actual macros.
+Other compiler paths (binary loads, pipeline compilation, function constants)
+are not counted. Future additions to compile options must update the fingerprint.
+
+`python3 tests/vulkan/check-shader-compiler-observer-native.py HEADERS LIB SOURCE`
+checks default-off behavior, repeated inputs, four concurrent cache clients,
+real GPU readback, macro/option distinctions and bounded-ledger saturation.
+This is diagnostic instrumentation, not a fix for the Detroit black screen.
+
+### Experimental Metal library optimization policy
+
+`env.MADEIRA_VK_METAL_LIBRARY_OPTIMIZATION = size` selects Metal's library
+size optimization for newly compiled MSL libraries. The default (unset or
+`default`) retains upstream runtime-speed optimization. Unknown values fall back
+to default. This is a process-wide setting: fully end the Wine session before
+changing it. It does not change SPIR-V, fast-math policy, advertised capabilities,
+cache limits, or shader compilation diagnostics. Supported Metal runtimes print
+`[vk-compiler] Metal library optimization=size` once when applying the option.
+An older runtime without the optimizationLevel selector retains its default.
+
+This is an A/B experiment, not an established Detroit speedup: early Mac
+measurements of two captured vertex shaders showed no stable improvement.
+Complex shaders and the iPad compiler may behave differently. Size optimization
+can reduce subsequent GPU performance. Keep the working game settings and cache
+intact; add only this setting for the experiment, then remove it to revert.
+
+Native verification observes the actual options passed to Metal without replacing
+the compiler, then checks real Vulkan compute specialization and GPU readback:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/vulkan/check-metal-library-options-native.py HEADERS LIBMOLTENVK
+```
+
+### Experimental integer fragment-output compatibility
+
+`env.MADEIRA_VK_INTEGER_FRAGMENT_OUTPUTS = 1` preserves float32 output bits
+when a scalar or vector fragment output is directed to an integer color
+attachment. It is off by default. Vulkan defines the mismatching components'
+values as undefined; this policy chooses a bit representation, not a guaranteed
+reconstruction of game intent. Test actual gameplay for artifacts.
+
+Only directly located 32-bit float scalar/vector outputs are adapted in a private
+compiler copy. Structs, arrays, component/secondary outputs and pointer aliases
+are not handled; alpha-to-coverage pipelines are excluded. Native integer outputs,
+float attachments, input shader files, fast math and capability bits are unchanged.
+
+Both signed and unsigned target masks are included in cache matching and archive
+serialization. The local cache UUID has an IO-v1 archive tag; older archives are
+rejected safely. The first launch of this package can therefore rebuild cached
+conversion results even with the option disabled. Do not delete user caches.
+
+`[vk-integer-output] remapped float32 output bits ...` reports up to the first
+eight conversions, while the enable message only reports policy selection.
+A removed Wine assertion or a null-pipeline guard is not considered a fix.
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/vulkan/check-integer-output-native.py HEADERS LIBMOLTENVK
+```
+
+This runs real Vulkan rendering/image readbacks across scalar/vector widths,
+values, float/uint/sint attachments and policy states, including native integer
+controls and cache export/import/merge. It is a compatibility test, not Vulkan
+conformance or proof of correct game images.
+
+The readback suite also checks rasterizer-discard pipelines with deliberately
+invalid pointers for ignored blend, viewport and multisample state. Vector
+readback checks the first component because the attachment format is R32.
+`tests/vulkan/integer-output-spirv-alias.cpp` is an additional SPIRV-Tools test:
+it validates real `OpCopyMemory` shaders and requires safe rejection when an
+adapted output pointer is either the source or destination. Build it with the
+MoltenVKShaderConverter and SPIRV-Tools include paths and `libSPIRVTools.a`.
diff --git a/build/vulkan-ios/backend.c b/build/vulkan-ios/backend.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cfcaee6097fd2f480e17b5d2cf5a44c93652b16b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/build/vulkan-ios/backend.c
@@ -0,0 +1,203 @@
+/* Static MoltenVK entry points for Wine's iOS Unix side.
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
+ * No handles, extensions, features or results are synthesized here.
+ */
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#include <vulkan/vulkan.h>
+#include "pipeline_diagnostics.h"
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL create_instance(const VkInstanceCreateInfo *info,
+        const VkAllocationCallbacks *allocator, VkInstance *instance)
+{
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vertex-layout-cache-isolation source=db66022459ffb663aa2b50f6b018bc2e124f5edf\n");
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] boundary capture + direct-cache-export fix\n");
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] bounded shader-library cache + owned merged specializations + opt-in compiler observer\n");
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] vertex-cache: reuse fully-covered inputs, isolate incomplete inputs\n");
+    const char *zero_inputs = getenv("MADEIRA_VK_ZERO_UNBOUND_VERTEX_INPUTS");
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] zero-unbound-vertex-inputs=%d (opt-in, real zero buffer)\n",
+            zero_inputs && !strcmp(zero_inputs, "1"));
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] batch fixes: vertex-cache, integer-blend-warning, fast-session-exit\n");
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] -> MoltenVK vkCreateInstance api=%#x\n",
+            info && info->pApplicationInfo ? info->pApplicationInfo->apiVersion : 0);
+    VkResult result = vkCreateInstance(info, allocator, instance);
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] <- MoltenVK vkCreateInstance result=%d instance=%p\n",
+            result, result == VK_SUCCESS ? (void *)*instance : NULL);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL enumerate_devices(VkInstance instance, uint32_t *count,
+        VkPhysicalDevice *devices)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices(instance, count, devices);
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices result=%d count=%u list=%d\n",
+            result, (result == VK_SUCCESS || result == VK_INCOMPLETE) ? *count : 0, devices != NULL);
+    if (devices && (result == VK_SUCCESS || result == VK_INCOMPLETE))
+        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < *count; i++)
+        {
+            VkPhysicalDeviceProperties props;
+            vkGetPhysicalDeviceProperties(devices[i], &props);
+            fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] physical-device[%u] name=\"%s\" vendor=%#x device=%#x api=%#x type=%u\n",
+                    i, props.deviceName, props.vendorID, props.deviceID, props.apiVersion, props.deviceType);
+        }
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL create_device(VkPhysicalDevice physical, const VkDeviceCreateInfo *info,
+        const VkAllocationCallbacks *allocator, VkDevice *device)
+{
+    /* Wine's generated client extension set does not expose portability_subset.
+     * Honor the native driver's mandatory extension without changing guest
+     * capabilities or enabling optional feature bits. VUID-pProperties-04451. */
+    const char *subset = "VK_KHR_portability_subset";
+    VkExtensionProperties *properties = NULL;
+    uint32_t count = 0;
+    VkResult result;
+    int required = 0, enabled = 0;
+    do
+    {
+        free(properties); properties = NULL;
+        result = vkEnumerateDeviceExtensionProperties(physical, NULL, &count, NULL);
+        if (result != VK_SUCCESS) return result;
+        if (!count) break;
+        properties = calloc(count, sizeof(*properties));
+        if (!properties) return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY;
+        result = vkEnumerateDeviceExtensionProperties(physical, NULL, &count, properties);
+    } while (result == VK_INCOMPLETE);
+    if (result != VK_SUCCESS) { free(properties); return result; }
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++)
+        if (!strcmp(properties[i].extensionName, subset)) required = 1;
+    free(properties);
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < info->enabledExtensionCount; i++)
+        if (!strcmp(info->ppEnabledExtensionNames[i], subset)) enabled = 1;
+    VkDeviceCreateInfo host = *info;
+    const char **extensions = NULL;
+    if (required && !enabled)
+    {
+        if (info->enabledExtensionCount == UINT32_MAX) return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY;
+        extensions = calloc((size_t)info->enabledExtensionCount + 1, sizeof(*extensions));
+        if (!extensions) return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY;
+        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < info->enabledExtensionCount; i++) extensions[i] = info->ppEnabledExtensionNames[i];
+        extensions[info->enabledExtensionCount] = subset;
+        host.enabledExtensionCount++;
+        host.ppEnabledExtensionNames = extensions;
+        fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] enabling native-required VK_KHR_portability_subset (no feature bits added)\n");
+    }
+    result = vkCreateDevice(physical, &host, allocator, device);
+    free(extensions);
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkCreateDevice result=%d\n", result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static void VKAPI_CALL clear_image(VkCommandBuffer cmd, VkImage image, VkImageLayout layout,
+        const VkClearColorValue *color, uint32_t count, const VkImageSubresourceRange *ranges)
+{
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkCmdClearColorImage (recording, execution not yet proven)\n");
+    vkCmdClearColorImage(cmd, image, layout, color, count, ranges);
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL queue_submit(VkQueue queue, uint32_t count, const VkSubmitInfo *submits,
+        VkFence fence)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkQueueSubmit(queue, count, submits, fence);
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkQueueSubmit count=%u result=%d\n", count, result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL wait_fences(VkDevice device, uint32_t count, const VkFence *fences,
+        VkBool32 all, uint64_t timeout)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkWaitForFences(device, count, fences, all, timeout);
+    fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkWaitForFences result=%d\n", result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL present_queue(VkQueue queue, const VkPresentInfoKHR *info)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkQueuePresentKHR(queue, info);
+    static unsigned long count;
+    unsigned long n = __atomic_add_fetch(&count, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+    if (n <= 8 || n % 120 == 0 || result != VK_SUCCESS)
+    {
+        fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkQueuePresentKHR call=%lu count=%u result=%d\n",
+                n, info->swapchainCount, result);
+        if (info->pResults)
+            for (uint32_t i = 0; i < info->swapchainCount; i++)
+                fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] present swapchain[%u] result=%d\n", i, info->pResults[i]);
+    }
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL create_swapchain(VkDevice device,
+        const VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR *info, const VkAllocationCallbacks *allocator,
+        VkSwapchainKHR *swapchain)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkCreateSwapchainKHR(device, info, allocator, swapchain);
+    static unsigned long count;
+    unsigned long n = __atomic_add_fetch(&count, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+    if (n <= 8 || n % 60 == 0 || result < 0)
+        fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK vkCreateSwapchainKHR call=%lu extent=%ux%u result=%d\n",
+                n, info->imageExtent.width, info->imageExtent.height, result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static void log_acquire(const char *name, VkResult result)
+{
+    static unsigned long count;
+    unsigned long n = __atomic_add_fetch(&count, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+    if (n <= 8 || n % 120 == 0 || result < 0)
+        fprintf(stderr, "[vk-host] MoltenVK %s call=%lu result=%d\n", name, n, result);
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL acquire_image(VkDevice device, VkSwapchainKHR swapchain,
+        uint64_t timeout, VkSemaphore semaphore, VkFence fence, uint32_t *index)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkAcquireNextImageKHR(device, swapchain, timeout, semaphore, fence, index);
+    log_acquire("vkAcquireNextImageKHR", result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static VkResult VKAPI_CALL acquire_image2(VkDevice device,
+        const VkAcquireNextImageInfoKHR *info, uint32_t *index)
+{
+    VkResult result = vkAcquireNextImage2KHR(device, info, index);
+    log_acquire("vkAcquireNextImage2KHR", result);
+    return result;
+}
+
+static PFN_vkVoidFunction VKAPI_CALL device_proc(VkDevice device, const char *name)
+{
+    PFN_vkVoidFunction proc = vkGetDeviceProcAddr(device, name);
+    if (!proc) return NULL;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCreateShaderModule")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)diag_create_shader_module;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkDestroyShaderModule")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)diag_destroy_shader_module;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCreateGraphicsPipelines")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)diag_create_graphics_pipelines;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkDestroyDevice")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)diag_destroy_device;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCreateSwapchainKHR")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)create_swapchain;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkAcquireNextImageKHR")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)acquire_image;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkAcquireNextImage2KHR")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)acquire_image2;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkQueuePresentKHR")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)present_queue;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCmdClearColorImage")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)clear_image;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkQueueSubmit")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)queue_submit;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkWaitForFences")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)wait_fences;
+    return proc;
+}
+
+static PFN_vkVoidFunction VKAPI_CALL instance_proc(VkInstance instance, const char *name)
+{
+    PFN_vkVoidFunction proc = vkGetInstanceProcAddr(instance, name);
+    if (!proc) return NULL;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCreateInstance")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)create_instance;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)enumerate_devices;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkCreateDevice")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)create_device;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkGetDeviceProcAddr")) return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)device_proc;
+    return proc;
+}
+
+void *madeira_vulkan_symbol(const char *name)
+{
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkGetInstanceProcAddr")) return (void *)instance_proc;
+    if (!strcmp(name, "vkGetDeviceProcAddr")) return (void *)device_proc;
+    return NULL;
+}
